{{also|ratte|rätte}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|ratte}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|ratta}}. There is still some uncertainty about the origin of the word and that of the animal itself, which was unknown to Europe in antiquity. Older etymological sources explain the [[Germanic]] words as borrowings from [[Romance]]. It is now widely accepted that this is not the case. The further etymology is unsettled. The word may go back to an unknown [[substrate]] language, or be formed from a [[Proto-Indo-European]] root meaning „to gnaw“ (compare {{cog|la|rōdere}}). The consonantism ''ratta'' in Old High German (instead of *''razza''), along with other hints, makes it likely that the former is borrowed from {{etyl|osx|de}} {{m|osx|ratta}}. The dialectal German variant {{m|de|Ratz}} is probably an expressive derivative, though it could be an Old High German adaptation of the Old Saxon form. See {{cog|en|rat}} for more. Compare also {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|de|rat}}, {{cog|sv|råtta}}, {{cog|fr|rat}}.

